WEBVTT

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:04.000
The Red tips

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
The Blue Planet

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
The Blue Planet

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.960
Ours is truly a blue planet.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.920
Water covers most of the world's surface.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.920
Here, we are the outsiders.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:57.120
But under the waves, one group of people

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.080
thrives.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.080
Fish.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.600
Fish are masters of the waters.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.400
And sailfish are the fastest of them all.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.880
Their speed makes them one of the ocean's most fearsome predators.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.880
Off the coast of Mexico,

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.640
30 sailfish have surrounded a ball of sardines.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:47.000
To catch their prey requires more than speed alone.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.440
To catch their prey requires more than speed alone.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.520
In the tightest tins, fins maintain stability.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.480
Their sickle-shaped tail powers them forwards

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:17.000
and that extraordinary dorsal fin helps intimidate their prey.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.480
To the naked eye, the action is too fast.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.120
Slowed, their challenge becomes clear.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.720
Just picking a target is hard enough.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.560
Knocking it off balance separates it from the show.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:58.400
This requires extraordinary skill.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.560
Not every attempt is successful.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.880
But as more sailfish join in,

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.080
when one misses, another takes its place.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.240
The shoal of sardines is methodical.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.320
It's a very difficult task.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.760
The shoal of sardines is methodically wiped out.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.280
Sailfish are top predators, very little threatens them.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.040
But for the majority of fish, this is not the case.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.720
For most fish, the open ocean is extremely dangerous.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.000
And some go to extraordinary lengths just to survive.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.520
Flying fish.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:22.280
Free of the water, they soar on elongated fins

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:24.800
leaving their predators far behind.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:30.240
Leaving their predators far behind.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:56.920
A flight of fish.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:04.160
Helping predators is not the only test facing the flying fish.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:11.360
They must also protect their developing young.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:18.480
These flying fish are searching for the one thing that will make this possible.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.600
In such a vast ocean, it's not easy.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:27.360
They're in luck.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.480
A palm frond.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:37.960
It's a tiny island adrift in a huge ocean.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:47.240
And like an island, it offers shelter not for the flying fish, but for their eggs.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:59.560
The females lay eggs on the raft where the males fertilize them.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:10.960
The first fish spawn and this triggers the others to start.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:22.200
Soon thousands jog in the melee.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:27.920
Innumerable strands of eggs are laid.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:49.280
The raft starts to tilt under their weight.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:07:04.920
The best place to lay eggs is right inside the frond.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:19.960
For some, the attempt proves fatal and living fish become entombed.

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:29.120
The raft starts to sink under the weight of so many eggs.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.080
But this is far from a disaster.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:38.520
Drinking away from the surface actually improves the eggs' chances of survival.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:48.080
In just a few days, having been safely hidden in the depths, they'll hatch out.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:51.920
Other fish protect their offspring in different ways.

00:07:51.920 --> 00:08:07.960
Some go to far greater lengths to care for them.

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:25.280
The shallow waters of southern Australia are home to many strange creatures.

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:44.320
It's a fairy tale world of seahorses, star gazers and stingrays.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:09:01.080
And none compare with the beauty of the weedy sea dragon.

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:19.480
The dragon's tiny fins beat frantically to prevent the current from sweeping it away.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:34.520
It's the beginning of spring, the season when sea dragons begin their courtship.

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:57.440
And in the evening light, they start to dance.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:16.920
In a graceful duet, each partner mirrors the actions of the other.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:46.840
Darkness will soon draw a veil over the pair, but they will dance on into the night.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:11:14.320
Two months later, and the result of their courtship is revealed.

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:20.240
That's the male, and he's the one that's carrying the eggs, with rows and rows of them

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:25.720
embedded in his tail.

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:29.880
That night, the female transferred her eggs to him.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:34.360
Since then, the male alone has cared for them.

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:48.240
By carrying them with him, he's kept them safe.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:56.320
And now it's time for his efforts to be rewarded.

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:10.200
The eggs are ready to hatch.

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:26.400
In the calm of a summer morning, a baby sea dragon with yolk sacs still attached is born.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:43.240
The weed bed shelters older dragons that are already able to feed themselves.

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:47.800
Although these dragons were well cared for by their father, now they must find their

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:54.480
own way in the world.

00:12:54.480 --> 00:13:04.240
There are fish, however, which provide their young with a safe refuge for far longer.

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:07.640
The southwestern Pacific.

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:15.920
A convict fish, and it's something of a marine architect.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:22.680
Underground, it has created a labyrinth of tunnels.

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:25.960
This adult never ventures out of its burrow.

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:34.080
What it eats is a mystery.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:38.320
But it doesn't live here alone.

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:42.800
At another entrance, faces peer out.

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:46.880
Juvenile convict fish.

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:54.960
Unlike their parent, the youngsters are not tied to the burrow.

00:13:54.960 --> 00:14:02.280
And as they start to emerge, a trickle becomes a flood of fish.

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:13.640
There are thousands of them.

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:28.960
And they all help with the chores.

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:52.880
Only hands make light work.

00:14:52.880 --> 00:15:08.960
The young fish swarm together, thousands of mouths gulping plankton.

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:16.720
What the adults eat must somehow involve these youngsters.

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:22.400
Whether the young feed their parents by regurgitating food or through some other mechanism, we just

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:25.000
don't know.

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:30.280
Whatever the answer, the youngsters provide their parents with a meal and in return get

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:44.400
a roof over their heads.

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:47.680
Producing young is just one challenge.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:55.840
Providing food and somewhere to live are further trials fish must face.

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:57.360
The Californian coast.

00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:02.640
A wide range of species live here.

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:20.840
But all this life means competition for living space is intense.

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:26.800
Old shells are highly prized.

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:33.560
And this one is occupied by a sarcastic fringe head.

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:37.240
These fish are exceptionally quarrelsome.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:17:02.040
They have to be to defend their living space.

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:25.920
Inadvertently it's wandered into the fringe head's territory and that can't be tolerated.

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:40.040
The octopus's impressive jab holds the fringe head at bay.

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:45.880
There is more to this behavior than being bad tempered.

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:51.640
The fringe head needs to defend its patch if it is to get enough to eat and the octopus

00:17:51.640 --> 00:18:03.040
was competition.

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:09.480
Crabs are not the easiest of mouthfuls.

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:15.720
Because of the shortage of living space, there are constant boundary disputes.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:18.960
Especially with other fringe heads.

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:28.040
But this one has got too close.

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:49.560
Despite the most extravagant threats, neither is prepared to back down.

00:18:49.560 --> 00:19:08.440
Success, and it's quick to get back to its shell.

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:11.080
A fringe head can never drop its guard.

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:19.240
There's too much competition.

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:37.800
Some fish have moved to places where they have fewer rivals.

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:39.280
A mud skipper.

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:45.120
A fish that spends most of its life out of the sea.

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:50.920
It can walk on land and breathe air.

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:59.680
Its life is very different from that of most fish.

00:19:59.680 --> 00:20:03.040
A fish out of water, maybe.

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:09.840
But they thrive here in Japan.

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:14.880
So what's made this upheaval worthwhile?

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:21.560
The answer lies in the mud.

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:26.920
As the tide retreats, it exposes mud flats.

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:33.960
Sunlight hits the rich silt and tiny plants and animals flourish there.

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:38.280
All food for a mud skipper.

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:41.480
But life on land is not without problems.

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:52.560
It's hard work to find a mate.

00:20:52.560 --> 00:21:14.160
Jumping high above the mud will get you noticed.

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:19.600
With eyes perched on the top of their heads, the mud skippers keep a lookout for both friend

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:22.880
and foe.

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:47.440
And males fight those who intrude on their territory.

00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:54.680
They must also take care not to dry out in the sun.

00:21:54.680 --> 00:22:02.640
Rolling in the ooze keeps the skin cool and moist.

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:13.920
For this smaller species, a better option is to retreat underground.

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:31.120
So he digs himself a tunnel down into the mud.

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:51.000
This heap of spoil is an indication of the extent of his excavations.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:23:04.240
With the tide flooding the tunnel twice a day, maintenance is a real burden.

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:07.400
The tunnel is more than a refuge from the sun.

00:23:07.400 --> 00:23:11.600
It serves another very important purpose.

00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:17.760
The tunnel is actually U-shaped and at the far end is a sealed chamber, the walls of

00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:23.320
which are lined with eggs.

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:29.280
The eggs are kept in air as it's richer in oxygen than the water.

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:33.800
The problem is the air that's trapped here won't last for long.

00:23:33.800 --> 00:23:39.800
So the male travels to the open end of the tunnel to gulp fresh air.

00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:46.240
Back he goes down his tunnel where he releases it into the egg chamber.

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:50.520
Actually replenishing the oxygen on which the eggs depend.

00:23:50.520 --> 00:24:04.040
He will repeat this hundreds and hundreds of times until his young hatch.

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:18.120
This lifestyle is very demanding, yet the mudskipper has found a way around every problem.

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:25.420
The harsh challenges of life in the ocean have encouraged other fish to leave the sea.

00:24:25.420 --> 00:24:30.360
Not for land, but for fresh water.

00:24:30.360 --> 00:24:36.560
Hawaii is the remotest island chain on the planet.

00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:42.000
These pools look the perfect place for the fish to live, secluded and free from competition

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:45.720
and predators.

00:24:45.720 --> 00:25:12.800
Yet few contain fish, for one very considerable reason.

00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:17.760
Surely no fish could swim up this.

00:25:17.760 --> 00:25:27.400
But one fish comes from the ocean intent on colonizing these streams.

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:35.360
It's a tiny goby and it's a rock climber.

00:25:35.360 --> 00:25:41.760
With pelvic fins fused into a disk which acts like a sucker, all the goby needs is a film

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:53.600
of water to climb through.

00:25:53.600 --> 00:26:17.480
The pioneer is soon followed by many others, possibly following its scent trail.

00:26:17.480 --> 00:26:30.160
They clamber on, ever upwards against the flow.

00:26:30.160 --> 00:26:46.040
Drops of water fall like bombs.

00:26:46.040 --> 00:26:52.880
False leads waste crucial energy.

00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:57.880
Some must rest.

00:26:57.880 --> 00:27:08.760
For others, the effort is just too much.

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:32.640
They die in their attempt to reach the top.

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:50.160
Against all the odds, a few heroic individuals do make it to the top.

00:27:50.160 --> 00:28:02.040
They find themselves in a near perfect fish habitat.

00:28:02.040 --> 00:28:11.400
Where the gobies can feed and grow and breed in peace.

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:17.800
In time, their own young will be swept downstream and out to sea and the cycle will begin all

00:28:17.800 --> 00:28:33.600
over again.

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:39.560
Fresh water presents particular challenges for fish.

00:28:39.560 --> 00:28:45.160
Nutrients can be in very short supply in spring water, so here fish must take every chance

00:28:45.160 --> 00:28:47.120
they can to find food.

00:28:47.120 --> 00:28:53.400
The rain that falls on these Kenyan hills percolates through the rocks.

00:28:53.400 --> 00:29:06.240
Finally, emerging as crystal clear pools.

00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:14.200
Pools that are home to fish, including barbel.

00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:29.800
The fish share these waters with all sorts of creatures.

00:29:29.800 --> 00:29:40.920
Including hippopotamus.

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:45.760
These giant vegetarians are no threat to the fish.

00:29:45.760 --> 00:29:55.680
In fact, they're key to their survival here.

00:29:55.680 --> 00:30:01.040
After a night of grazing on land, these hippos return to spend the daylight hours in the

00:30:01.040 --> 00:30:16.880
cooling waters.

00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:23.040
And the barbel come to meet them.

00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:36.520
Soon, each hippo is trailed by a shoal of fish, waiting for their breakfast.

00:30:36.520 --> 00:30:46.240
Hippo droppings.

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:54.520
But it's not just the hippos done the fish are interested in.

00:30:54.520 --> 00:31:04.080
When the hippos reach one particular spot in the pool, they stand still and wait.

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:11.000
And the fish start to clean them.

00:31:11.000 --> 00:31:18.360
Removing ticks, parasites and other tasty morsels.

00:31:18.360 --> 00:31:27.040
To the fish, the hippos are a mobile cafeteria.

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:33.720
The hippos seem to be enjoying the sensation.

00:31:33.720 --> 00:31:47.880
The only thing that interrupts the feast is the need to take an occasional breath.

00:31:47.880 --> 00:31:52.640
Cleaning the hippo's skin was just the hors d'oeuvre.

00:31:52.640 --> 00:31:59.040
Now it's time for the main course.

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:09.280
So, in addition to providing skin care, the fish look after the hippo's dental hygiene.

00:32:09.280 --> 00:32:19.280
It's an arrangement that suits both parties.

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:24.320
But perhaps it's the fish that are the overall winners.

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:29.480
For thanks to the hippos, they are able to feed on the abundant vegetation that would

00:32:29.480 --> 00:32:38.440
otherwise be beyond their reach, growing around their pool on land.

00:32:38.440 --> 00:32:44.800
Providing a cleaning service is clearly a good way of getting a meal.

00:32:44.800 --> 00:33:03.160
And there is cleaning to be done in the sea as well.

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:14.080
The life of this wrasse is centred on removing parasites from other reef fish.

00:33:14.080 --> 00:33:15.080
Cleaning.

00:33:15.080 --> 00:33:22.560
Predatory jacks.

00:33:22.560 --> 00:33:28.200
Normally they would snap up such a little fish, but this is an established relationship

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:35.680
and both sides know the rules.

00:33:35.680 --> 00:33:40.760
With so many jacks and only a few wrasse in attendance, not all the jacks are going to

00:33:40.760 --> 00:33:46.200
get cleaned.

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:52.040
But all this life attracts other predators.

00:33:52.040 --> 00:34:10.020
Silver tip sharks.

00:34:10.020 --> 00:34:15.960
The reef provides shelter for the smaller fish, but the jacks remain exposed.

00:34:15.960 --> 00:34:22.680
Yet this may be a chance for the jacks to solve their cleaning problem.

00:34:22.680 --> 00:34:31.000
They've spotted an opportunity.

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:45.960
Sharks have skin like sandpaper.

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:51.680
And bumping into the shark's flanks helps the jacks to rid themselves of parasites and

00:34:51.680 --> 00:34:53.680
dead skin.

00:34:53.680 --> 00:34:59.800
Perhaps the jacks find this a more effective alternative to the cleaner fish.

00:34:59.800 --> 00:35:03.960
And soon swarms of jacks pursue the sharks.

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:10.880
All itching to have a scratch.

00:35:10.880 --> 00:35:15.640
Unsurprisingly all this attention bothers the sharks.

00:35:15.640 --> 00:35:21.960
And they head back to the blue water.

00:35:21.960 --> 00:35:35.600
Leaving the residents of the reef to resume life as normal.

00:35:35.600 --> 00:35:39.360
Coral reefs are the richest habitats on earth.

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:44.360
It's not surprising that with so many different kinds of animals living so closely together,

00:35:44.360 --> 00:35:51.720
some extraordinary relationships have evolved.

00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:59.180
A clownfish, a small and defenseless resident of the reef.

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:07.120
It seems to have picked a tough place to live amongst the tentacles of a sea anemone.

00:36:07.120 --> 00:36:13.680
Each tentacle is armed with paralyzing stings that can kill a fish.

00:36:13.680 --> 00:36:17.920
Yet the clownfish are totally immune.

00:36:17.920 --> 00:36:23.080
For this pair, the anemone is like a castle.

00:36:23.080 --> 00:36:27.060
So long as they stay surrounded by the tentacles, they're safe.

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:35.300
And so this is where they choose to lay their eggs.

00:36:35.300 --> 00:36:51.680
After carefully selecting the site, work begins on preparing the surface.

00:36:51.680 --> 00:36:57.920
Both fish share in the labor, though it's the larger female who decides when all is

00:36:57.920 --> 00:37:01.280
ready.

00:37:01.280 --> 00:37:20.600
Lines and lines of tiny eggs are stuck to the rock and then fertilized.

00:37:20.600 --> 00:37:26.680
They're laid so close to the anemone, they will be safe.

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:34.680
And for the next seven days, they'll receive constant care, much of which is provided by

00:37:34.680 --> 00:37:38.120
the male.

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:42.960
His seemingly obsessive concern for the eggs is for good reason.

00:37:42.960 --> 00:37:48.860
His position in the anemone is far from secure.

00:37:48.860 --> 00:37:52.000
The female watches his every move.

00:37:52.000 --> 00:37:56.720
She's in charge here, and if his efforts don't match up to her standards, she'll get rid

00:37:56.720 --> 00:38:03.360
of him.

00:38:03.360 --> 00:38:21.360
In line to take over are a host of immature clownfish, each waiting to move up the hierarchy.

00:38:21.360 --> 00:38:26.720
For the male, the best way to stay in the female's favor is by lavishing care on the

00:38:26.720 --> 00:38:30.440
eggs.

00:38:30.440 --> 00:38:39.280
So he focuses all his efforts on keeping them clean and healthy.

00:38:39.280 --> 00:38:45.760
The eggs grow rapidly, and soon their tiny beating hearts are visible.

00:38:45.760 --> 00:39:00.040
It seems he's done a good job.

00:39:00.040 --> 00:39:05.640
Clownfish can hide away within an anemone.

00:39:05.640 --> 00:39:09.600
But most fish don't have this option.

00:39:09.600 --> 00:39:21.000
For some, the only way of avoiding danger is by hiding amongst their own kind.

00:39:21.000 --> 00:39:34.000
In shoals.

00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:40.920
Packed close together, no one anchovy stands out.

00:39:40.920 --> 00:39:46.080
By sensing and reacting to the movements of their immediate neighbors, thousands can move

00:39:46.080 --> 00:39:55.200
as one.

00:39:55.200 --> 00:40:17.720
For a predator, picking out an individual becomes nearly impossible.

00:40:17.720 --> 00:40:36.720
The shoal's unity is its strength.

00:40:36.720 --> 00:40:56.600
Yet each fish is acting from selfish motives.

00:40:56.600 --> 00:41:02.200
Moving together, the fish confuse the sea lions so much that they leave to look elsewhere

00:41:02.200 --> 00:41:06.240
for a smaller, less tricky target.

00:41:06.240 --> 00:41:11.960
The sheer size of the shoal defeated the sea lions, but there are fish that can overcome

00:41:11.960 --> 00:41:15.800
such strategies.

00:41:15.800 --> 00:41:22.040
Off the coast of South Africa, this huge shoal of sardines is shadowed by a ragged toothed

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:29.280
shark.

00:41:29.280 --> 00:41:36.000
Other sharks join the menacing escort.

00:41:36.000 --> 00:41:41.600
The shoal has been driven into the shallows by a cold ocean current.

00:41:41.600 --> 00:41:50.320
And this gives the sharks an opportunity.

00:41:50.320 --> 00:41:57.320
Hundreds have moved into position.

00:41:57.320 --> 00:41:59.080
Sharks have a special sense.

00:41:59.080 --> 00:42:09.360
They can detect the electrical signals their prey gives off when it moves.

00:42:09.360 --> 00:42:16.520
It's a sixth sense that can give them an edge.

00:42:16.520 --> 00:42:21.600
But with hundreds of thousands of fish crammed into the shallows, the sharks need only rely

00:42:21.600 --> 00:42:28.040
on their speed and agility.

00:42:28.040 --> 00:42:41.960
As the first shark starts to hunt in earnest, a feeding frenzy breaks out.

00:42:41.960 --> 00:42:43.840
The shoal's defenses are weakened.

00:42:43.840 --> 00:42:49.080
There isn't enough space to maneuver.

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:58.200
And the sharks can gorge themselves.

00:42:58.200 --> 00:43:03.480
Despite the casualties, the shoal is so vast that the sharks have little effect on its

00:43:03.480 --> 00:43:12.960
size.

00:43:12.960 --> 00:43:18.120
Fish not only come together in great shoals for defense, but at other critical times in

00:43:18.120 --> 00:43:22.920
their lives when they're ready to spawn.

00:43:22.920 --> 00:43:33.600
These events only occur for a few days each year.

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:38.560
Snapper are normally solitary, but they've traveled here from hundreds of miles away

00:43:38.560 --> 00:43:45.200
to gather off the coast of Belize.

00:43:45.200 --> 00:43:58.560
Along this one reef, kubera, dog, and mutton snapper form huge shoals.

00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:04.240
Sixty meters down, there is an eerie coolness to the scene.

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:16.360
But things are about to hot up.

00:44:16.360 --> 00:44:19.120
It's the evening of the full moon.

00:44:19.120 --> 00:44:23.760
The tides are just right.

00:44:23.760 --> 00:44:32.000
A great column of fish leaves the bottom.

00:44:32.000 --> 00:44:46.480
As they rise through the water, small groups break free of the shoal.

00:44:46.480 --> 00:44:55.080
Each burst is led by a female, with the males racing behind.

00:44:55.080 --> 00:45:14.280
As she sheds her eggs, they add their sperm to the mix.

00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:19.000
By synchronizing the time when they gather together, the maximum numbers of fish can

00:45:19.000 --> 00:45:27.560
join in this mass spawning.

00:45:27.560 --> 00:45:44.560
Millions of fertilized eggs are released, cast into the ocean currents, and straight

00:45:44.560 --> 00:45:53.440
into a dangerous world.

00:45:53.440 --> 00:46:05.600
Whale sharks, the largest fish on earth.

00:46:05.600 --> 00:46:15.040
That shark might weigh 10 tons, yet they feed on the tiniest creatures, including snapper

00:46:15.040 --> 00:46:25.840
eggs.

00:46:25.840 --> 00:46:35.720
Life is being created, sustained, and destroyed simultaneously in one huge event.

00:46:35.720 --> 00:46:45.200
The struggle for life encapsulated into a single moment.

00:46:45.200 --> 00:46:50.560
The oceans are perilous places to live, yet fish have developed the most extraordinary

00:46:50.560 --> 00:46:55.400
means for survival.

00:46:55.400 --> 00:47:01.160
Their astounding diversity, the product of millions of years of evolution, has enabled

00:47:01.160 --> 00:47:04.240
them to triumph.

00:47:04.240 --> 00:47:25.920
Dominating the one habitat that we have so far failed to make our own.

00:47:25.920 --> 00:47:45.920
Filming underwater raised all sorts of problems for the life team.

00:47:45.920 --> 00:47:50.720
Not least of which was that they were only able to experience the underwater world for

00:47:50.720 --> 00:47:56.720
as long as the air on their backs or in their lungs held out.

00:47:56.720 --> 00:48:01.640
But over three years, the team were lucky enough to capture on film some extraordinary

00:48:01.640 --> 00:48:17.080
moments in the lives of fish.

00:48:17.080 --> 00:48:29.920
The waters off the Yucatan Peninsula in Mexico are a rich hunting ground for sailfish.

00:48:29.920 --> 00:48:35.560
Cameraman Rick Rosenthal has teamed up with sport fishing captain Anthony Mandillo to

00:48:35.560 --> 00:48:42.760
try and film the hunting behavior of these amazing animals.

00:48:42.760 --> 00:48:47.520
Seeing birds lead them to the sailfish.

00:48:47.520 --> 00:49:10.960
Getting to the bait balls and into the water quickly is key before the feast is consumed.

00:49:10.960 --> 00:49:14.400
It's all very well telling Rick to hurry.

00:49:14.400 --> 00:49:19.960
But these fish are capable of swimming at over 60 miles an hour.

00:49:19.960 --> 00:49:41.560
Keeping up with them is hard enough.

00:49:41.560 --> 00:49:46.240
Getting right in amongst the action is vital, but Rick has to try to avoid becoming part

00:49:46.240 --> 00:49:49.520
of it.

00:49:49.520 --> 00:49:54.800
Fills nearly one meter long, scything through the water at breakneck speed, are guaranteed

00:49:54.800 --> 00:50:06.800
to get the heart racing.

00:50:06.800 --> 00:50:20.080
But Rick holds his nerve as the sailfish pick off sardine off the sardine right in front

00:50:20.080 --> 00:50:23.040
of him.

00:50:23.040 --> 00:50:26.480
Almost as soon as it started, it was all over.

00:50:26.480 --> 00:50:40.000
I had a wild seashell out there today, really wild, must have been 50 sailfish or 49, but

00:50:40.000 --> 00:50:44.400
very aggressive fish, very hungry, everybody on the move.

00:50:44.400 --> 00:50:48.360
And I had to just keep kicking and kicking and kicking and kicking and kicking and keeping

00:50:48.360 --> 00:50:49.360
the action.

00:50:49.360 --> 00:50:56.960
And after a while the sardine patch was eaten up to just a little sliver and then it was

00:50:56.960 --> 00:50:57.960
over.

00:50:57.960 --> 00:51:03.080
Nearly 2000 miles away on the other side of the Caribbean, another crew is taking a slightly

00:51:03.080 --> 00:51:04.480
different approach.

00:51:04.480 --> 00:51:10.200
They are trying to film flying fish.

00:51:10.200 --> 00:51:15.760
The team sets out at dawn on the Hog Snapper, a commercial fishing boat.

00:51:15.760 --> 00:51:33.560
Conditions are in stark contrast to the gleaming sport fishing boat in Mexico.

00:51:33.560 --> 00:51:38.480
They are hoping to use the local fisherman's expertise to put them in the right pace at

00:51:38.480 --> 00:51:39.480
the right time.

00:51:39.480 --> 00:51:53.840
It's not a big boat and the crew's bedroom has now become the kitchen.

00:51:53.840 --> 00:52:15.960
Rather than racing around the ocean chasing the action, the flying fish team have to sit

00:52:15.960 --> 00:52:21.680
it out and wait for the fish to come to them.

00:52:21.680 --> 00:52:26.160
The flying fish will spawn onto debris in the water and the team tie onto a floating

00:52:26.160 --> 00:52:32.520
palm frond to try and make sure they are close by in case the action begins.

00:52:32.520 --> 00:52:48.760
And sure enough they don't have long to wait.

00:52:48.760 --> 00:53:00.760
Hundreds of fish have massed below the surface all intent on reaching the frond.

00:53:00.760 --> 00:53:28.840
And the frond is not the only thing that they are trying to lay their eggs on.

00:53:28.840 --> 00:53:34.320
The weight of the eggs sinks the palm frond and puts an end to the spawning and to the

00:53:34.320 --> 00:53:35.320
crew's filming.

00:53:35.320 --> 00:53:52.360
There are moments like that we do the job for.

00:53:52.360 --> 00:53:53.360
Everything was right.

00:53:53.360 --> 00:53:54.360
The light was right.

00:53:54.360 --> 00:54:03.680
The four tonnes of flying fish all going mental.

00:54:03.680 --> 00:54:07.080
But now the fish's attention has turned to something bigger.

00:54:07.080 --> 00:54:11.160
They're spawning directly onto the boat.

00:54:11.160 --> 00:54:14.280
Barry is worried as he drags up a huge sheet of eggs.

00:54:14.280 --> 00:54:19.480
I clean this off like five minutes ago.

00:54:19.480 --> 00:54:24.280
Right now the problem is there are too many flying fish around.

00:54:24.280 --> 00:54:29.480
If we go through the night with the lights on and stuff more and more and more will keep

00:54:29.480 --> 00:54:33.480
coming and what they're doing here is they're actually laying on the boat now.

00:54:33.480 --> 00:54:37.280
So the boat has become their object and that is not good.

00:54:37.280 --> 00:54:40.720
So basically you're worried that if we just stay on this drift we're going to sink the

00:54:40.720 --> 00:54:41.720
boat.

00:54:41.720 --> 00:54:44.720
Five hours from now that will be 3,000 pounds.

00:54:44.720 --> 00:54:47.720
In the back here they will sink the boat.

00:54:47.720 --> 00:54:50.720
OK so we've got to leave it tonight.

00:54:50.720 --> 00:54:53.040
We can't stay here.

00:54:53.040 --> 00:54:57.200
Just five minutes of spawning has produced this.

00:54:57.200 --> 00:55:01.680
The team have no option but to move on.

00:55:01.680 --> 00:55:05.820
The next day the search for flying fish begins all over again.

00:55:05.820 --> 00:55:09.560
This time the team want to film the fish doing what they're famous for.

00:55:09.560 --> 00:55:14.520
We've got some lovely shots of them spawning but now they're really hard bit of trying

00:55:14.520 --> 00:55:15.520
to get them flying.

00:55:15.520 --> 00:55:20.760
It's going to be a good challenge.

00:55:20.760 --> 00:55:29.640
The fish are around but they're all too far off to film.

00:55:29.640 --> 00:55:32.360
The action is impressive this morning.

00:55:32.360 --> 00:55:36.800
Unpredictable but impressive but it's distant and it's not happening next to the boat today.

00:55:36.800 --> 00:55:47.680
They're either being chased off by something or they're just not interested.

00:55:47.680 --> 00:56:02.320
The next day the crew decides to try a different approach.

00:56:02.320 --> 00:56:15.440
Now they're just where they need to be but it puts them directly in the firing line.

00:56:15.440 --> 00:56:22.200
Despite being bombarded their strategy is paying off.

00:56:22.200 --> 00:56:24.200
That was amazing.

00:56:24.200 --> 00:56:28.600
We spent a long time in that wee boat today.

00:56:28.600 --> 00:56:31.320
The last two hours were just off the scale.

00:56:31.320 --> 00:56:32.320
We were just getting shot after shot.

00:56:32.320 --> 00:56:39.040
We need to get away to watching the big monitor but that felt really good.

00:56:39.040 --> 00:56:41.600
And Doug is right.

00:56:41.600 --> 00:56:43.720
It worked.

00:56:43.720 --> 00:56:47.720
Fishing fish taking to the air and flying.

00:56:47.720 --> 00:57:11.760
Slowed down 40 times.

00:57:11.760 --> 00:57:16.800
By working with people more used to catching fish than filming them the life team have

00:57:16.800 --> 00:57:43.800
been able to gain a unique insight into the hidden world of fish.
